    <!--
        The <database> entries are load-bearing, not decoration.

        Nextcloud's OC\DB\MigrationService sets `checkOracle = true` for any app
        whose <dependencies> block declares NO <database> at all. With that flag
        on, every migration is validated against ensureOracleConstraints(), whose
        Oracle-derived identifier limits Keepiq's schema does not satisfy:

          - the rule rejects a default-named primary key on any table whose
            unprefixed name is >= 23 chars — which still covers 10 of this
            app's 33 tables (keepiq_certificate_metadata,
            keepiq_passkey_credentials, keepiq_team_folder_members,
            keepiq_app_lease_policies, …).

        The companion "table name is too long" failure no longer applies. It
        used to: `doriath_certificate_metadata` was 28 chars unprefixed against
        a limit of 27. The doriath -> keepiq rename took one character off every
        table name, so the longest is now `keepiq_certificate_metadata` at
        exactly 27 and the table-name check passes. The primary-key rule above
        is what keeps these <database> entries load-bearing.

        The result was that `occ app:enable keepiq` ABORTED on a fresh install:
        the app was not installable from scratch on a stock Nextcloud at all.
        (It survived on long-lived dev instances only because the checks apply
        exclusively to tables that do not exist yet.)

        Declaring the databases the app actually supports is Nextcloud's own
        mechanism for this, and it is simply true: Keepiq has never supported
        Oracle, and its docker-compose, CI and every deployment run PostgreSQL,
        MySQL/MariaDB or SQLite. It also brings this app in line with the rest of
        the fleet — openregister and opencatalogi already ship exactly these
        three entries.

        Renaming a dozen shipped tables to satisfy Oracle limits an Oracle-less
        app will never hit would be a far larger and riskier change for no
        behavioural gain.

        THE `doriath_` PREFIX IS GONE, AND `doriath_` SHOULD NOT REAPPEAR.
        This app was renamed doriath -> keepiq (2026-08). The app id, namespace,
        routes and display name moved first; the storage identifiers followed
        (2026-09) once it was clear that leaving them behind meant carrying a
        dead codename in the schema for the life of the product.

        The schema half is 33 tables, 78 named indexes, 33 primary-key indexes
        and 2 sequences, moved by OCA\Keepiq\Repair\RenameDoriathTables — see
        the note on <repair-steps> below for why it is a PRE-migration step and
        not a migration. ALTER TABLE .. RENAME TO relinks a table in place, so
        no row is copied and none of the sensitive material in `keepiq_secrets`,
        `keepiq_enc_suites` (RSA-4096 private keys, AES-wrapped), `keepiq_ca_certs`,
        `keepiq_passkey_credentials` or `keepiq_attachments` is rewritten.

        The four ICache namespaces moved with it — keepiq_jwt_jti,
        keepiq_jwt_token, keepiq_cxp_relay, keepiq_breach_range. Each starts
        empty on the deploy that carries the change, which costs one bounded
        window: 360s in which a spent jti replays once more, 300s of live bearer
        tokens 401-ing, in-flight CXP handshakes dropped, and a cold HIBP cache.
        Accepted deliberately while the app still ships a not-production-ready
        disclaimer. The per-constant docblocks carry the detail.

        A `doriath_` string appearing in this repository now is a leftover, not
        a storage identifier, and should be fixed rather than preserved.
    -->
    <dependencies>
        <php min-version="8.3"/>
        <database min-version="10">pgsql</database>
        <database>sqlite</database>
        <database min-version="8.0">mysql</database>
        <!--
            Floor is 32, fleet-wide, so PHP 8.3 is guaranteed by the platform.

            31 was never actually deliverable: the CI matrix already tests only
            stable32 (see nextcloud-test-refs in .github/workflows/
            code-quality.yml), because OpenRegister — which this app builds on —
            declares `<nextcloud min-version="32" max-version="34"/>` itself. Advertising 31 in
            the App Store promised a range no test leg covered and no dependency
            could satisfy.

            max-version stays 34, which is the fleet-wide value everywhere
            except openconnector (35).
        -->
        <nextcloud min-version="32" max-version="34"/>
    </dependencies>

    <background-jobs>
        <job>OCA\Keepiq\BackgroundJob\RenewIntermediateCertificate</job>
        <job>OCA\Keepiq\BackgroundJob\CheckRootCertificateExpiry</job>
        <job>OCA\Keepiq\BackgroundJob\PurgeAuditLogJob</job>
        <job>OCA\Keepiq\BackgroundJob\ExpireSecretRequestsJob</job>
